### remove

Move a file, directory or symlink to the trash, or delete it with `permanent=true`. Either way the path is snapshotted first, so `undo` can bring it back. Symlinks are removed as symlinks, never followed. The trash uses `trash` or `trash-put`; when neither is installed or the command fails, nothing is removed and `permanent=true` is the way to remove the path. Anything over 512 MiB is refused rather than trashed; confirm with the user before deleting that with `permanent=true`.

---

### patch

Replace exact text in one file.
- Read the file with `read` before editing it; editing a file this conversation has not read is refused. The `12:` prefix in read output is not file content; only the text after it is.
- `old_string` must match exactly once unless `replace_all` is set. When it is not in the file exactly, it is matched again with smart quotes, Unicode dashes, no-break spaces and trailing whitespace read as plain ASCII; the file keeps its own characters wherever your edit left `old_string` unchanged. With `replace_all`, this fallback runs only when there is no exact occurrence. A miss reports the closest line in the file.
- Pass `edits` (a list of `{old_string, new_string, replace_all}`) for several replacements in one call: they apply in order, each seeing the previous result, and all are validated before anything is written, so a failed call leaves the file untouched and names the offending edit. Never combine `edits` with the scalar `old_string`/`new_string`/`replace_all` form.
- Line endings in `old_string`/`new_string` are matched and written in the file's own style (LF or CRLF).
- After the write, the file is parsed locally and any syntax errors the edit introduced are reported as a warning.
- One call is one `undo` step.
- Use `write` for new files or a deliberate full rewrite.


---

### undo

Restore one path to its latest snapshot. Every tool that changes a file snapshots it first; one `patch` call, even with several edits, is one snapshot.
- Restores file contents and permission modes, symlink targets, whole directory trees, and absence: undoing the creation of a file deletes it. Ownership, timestamps and extended attributes are not restored.
- Each successful restore pops one snapshot, so repeated calls walk further back. A filesystem error keeps the snapshot for a retry. An unusable snapshot is discarded with an error without changing the path; call again to reach an older entry.
- Snapshots belong to this conversation and survive a restart of it; the store keeps the newest 100 entries (64 MiB). This is not git and does not touch repository history.

---

### shell

Run one command with `/bin/bash` and return its exit code, stdout and stderr. Under bash and zsh a pipeline fails when any stage fails (`pipefail`), so `false | cat` reports exit 1. The working directory is `cwd`, default `/home/user/project`; set `cwd` instead of using `cd`.

Long output comes back as its head and its tail, with a marker between them naming the bytes left out and the file that holds the whole stream:

    [truncated: output budget (… bytes) reached; stdout bytes 25000-980000 of 1004000 (955000 bytes) are not shown; the whole stdout is at /path/shell-3-….log — read it on with range {"start_byte": 25000}]

Read any part of that file with `read` and a byte range, so do not pipe through `head` or `tail` just to shrink the output. Stdin is `/dev/null` and there is no terminal: a command that reads input gets end-of-file, and a password or confirmation prompt gets no answer.

A command does not have to finish before you get an answer. The call waits `timeout` seconds (default 30); a command that finishes inside that window comes back whole, and one still running comes back as a handle with whatever it has printed so far:

    command still running after 30s (handle 3, pid 4242). ...
    HANDLE 3 RUNNING

The command is never killed by the wait. Then `action="poll", handle="3"` returns new output and whether it is still running, `action="wait", handle="3", timeout=N` blocks up to N more seconds, and `action="kill", handle="3"` stops it (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, to its whole process group). `handle` defaults to the most recent running job. Do a poll before assuming a long build or test run has failed. Interrupting the turn kills a command the call is still waiting on.

The command gets a filtered environment: the configured allow-list (by default PATH, HOME, USER, LANG, LC_ALL, TERM, PWD, SHELL) plus the names you list in `env`. When the command references a variable that was filtered out, the result says so.

- Independent commands can go in parallel calls in one response; chain dependent ones with `&&` in one call.
- Search with `fs_search`, not `grep`, `rg`, or `find`.
- Read files with `read`, not `cat`, `head`, or `tail`.
- Edit files with `patch`, not `sed` or `awk`.
- Write files with `write`, not redirects or heredocs.
- Delete with `remove`, not `rm`.


